## Shared Derivation

The real square root is nonnegative. Therefore
`sqrt(x^2)=|x|`, which equals `-x` when `x<0`, `x` when `x>0`,
and `0` when `x=0`.

In a and b the original denominator is `x`, so zero is excluded before
simplification. In c and d the square root exists for every real x, and
there is no denominator: zero is included.

## a. y = sqrt(x^2)/x

Domain: `(-infinity,0) union (0,infinity)`. Range: `{-1,1}`.

For `x<0`, `y=(-x)/x=-1`. For `x>0`, `y=x/x=1`.
Draw the horizontal ray `y=-1` to the left of zero and the horizontal ray
`y=1` to the right. Mark open circles at `(0,-1)` and `(0,1)`.
There is no value at zero and no filled point anywhere on the y-axis.
Do not connect the two rays. There is no intersection with either axis.

Exact plotting points:

| x | -3 | -2 | -1 | -1/2 | 1/2 | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| y | -1 | -1 | -1 | -1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |

The one-sided limits are -1 and 1. These are two excluded ray endpoints
at a jump, not a single removable hole. No half-parabola occurs here.

## b. y = -2*sqrt(x^2)/x

Domain: `(-infinity,0) union (0,infinity)`. Range: `{-2,2}`.

For `x<0`, `y=-2*(-1)=2`. For `x>0`, `y=-2*1=-2`.
Equivalently, reflect a across the x-axis and double all vertical distances.
Draw `y=2` only to the left and `y=-2` only to the right.
Mark open circles at `(0,2)` and `(0,-2)`. Zero is undefined.
Do not connect the rays or insert a filled point on the y-axis.
There is no intersection with either axis.

Exact plotting points:

| x | -3 | -2 | -1 | -1/2 | 1/2 | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| y | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | -2 | -2 | -2 | -2 |

The one-sided limits are 2 and -2. Again these are two excluded endpoints
at a jump, not one removable hole. No half-parabola occurs here.

## c. y = x*sqrt(x^2)

Domain and range: all real numbers.

For `x<0`, `y=x*(-x)=-x^2`. For `x>0`, `y=x*x=x^2`.
At zero the original expression gives `0*sqrt(0)=0`.
Thus retain the left half of the downward-opening parabola `y=-x^2`
and the right half of the upward-opening parabola `y=x^2`.
Both retained halves include their common filled point `(0,0)`, drawn once.

Exact plotting points:

| x | -3 | -2 | -1 | -1/2 | 0 | 1/2 | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| y | -9 | -4 | -1 | -1/4 | 0 | 1/4 | 1 | 4 | 9 |

Plot these points and trace smooth parabolic branches, not straight
segments. The left branch lies in quadrant III and extends down-left;
the right branch lies in quadrant I and extends up-right.
Do not draw the other halves of either parent parabola.
Both one-sided limits equal the included value zero. The branches meet
continuously at the origin with horizontal tangent and no hole.
The origin is the only intersection with either axis.
The graph is odd and strictly increasing.

## d. y = -x*sqrt(x^2)

Domain and range: all real numbers.

For `x<0`, `y=-x*(-x)=x^2`. For `x>0`, `y=-x*x=-x^2`.
At zero the original expression gives zero.
Retain the left half of the upward-opening parabola `y=x^2`
and the right half of the downward-opening parabola `y=-x^2`.
Include the common filled origin once. This reflects c across the x-axis.

Exact plotting points:

| x | -3 | -2 | -1 | -1/2 | 0 | 1/2 | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| y | 9 | 4 | 1 | 1/4 | 0 | -1/4 | -1 | -4 | -9 |

Trace the smooth parabolic branches through these points. The left half
lies in quadrant II and extends up-left; the right half lies in quadrant IV
and extends down-right. Omit the unretained halves of the parent parabolas.
The branches meet continuously at the included origin with horizontal
tangent, not an open circle. The origin is the only intersection with
either axis. The graph is odd and strictly decreasing.

